# Source redundancy, second batch: the cases that take redundant_page to 50 (34 cases).
#
# Only the new cases live here; the original 16 stay in `retrieval.jsonl` so the run of
# 2026-09-24 and its recording stay reproducible as published.
#
# This point now has a second reason to be measured properly. The fetch-heavy A/B pilot of
# 2026-09-24 (benchmarks/ab/results/2026-09-24-scattered-piloto) asked it six times and it
# dropped nothing: every page it was shown genuinely added something, so the end-to-end run
# measured the agent's search-path variance and not the lever. Decision-level accuracy is the
# only evidence this point has, which makes the quality of these cases the whole argument.
#
# The distinction the point exists to make, and the one every hard case here turns on:
#
#   drop: the page's informational content is already in `known`. Reprints, syndicated wire
#         copy under another masthead, a summary of a document the agent already has, the
#         same figures with the same attribution, and pages carrying no facts at all.
#   keep: anything that moves the purpose forward, however slightly. A new attribution when
#         the purpose is corroboration; a qualification, a correction or a later revision of
#         a known figure; a breakdown of a known total; the same event seen from the part of
#         the purpose still unanswered; a denial or a dissent.
#
# The failure mode this batch is built to expose is the one that costs coverage: judging
# 'same topic' instead of 'adds nothing'. That is exactly the drift that forced the
# relabelling of rd-04 and rd-12 in the first batch, so half the keeps below are pages that
# look like restatements and carry one new thing.
#
# rd-39 and rd-44 are declared negative controls: both turn on which of two dates is later,
# which this model class reads as text (paper Section 5.3).
#
# RELABELLED after the first run, on the question's own criteria and not because the model
# disagreed: rd-19, rd-23, rd-35 and rd-42 were authored as `drop` on the theory that a page
# with no bearing on the purpose adds nothing. They were wrong. The question asks whether the
# text carries "no new figure, date, name, outcome, qualification or source" that `known`
# lacks, and all four carry several - a magnitude and a depth, a founding year and a
# headcount, a fleet size, a set of metro lines. They are off-purpose, not redundant, and
# `decide_redundancy` is never given a relevance signal to judge that with: dropping an
# off-topic page is page triage's job and triage runs first. The original labels were the
# annotator making one point do two jobs. Both sets of numbers are published in
# `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty/`: 22/34 before the correction and 26/34 after, with the
# evaluator's answers untouched.
#
# rd-15 in the first batch stays `drop` and is not the same case: it carries no fact of any
# kind, only earthquake safety advice, so it satisfies the question's own criterion.
#
# DISPUTED, recorded and not resolved: the blind second annotator labelled rd-42 `drop`,
# against the correction above and without having seen either label. It is the weakest of the
# four: a page explaining how to reach a building by metro neither restates the known address
# nor carries facts bearing on it, so it fits neither side of the question cleanly. rd-19,
# rd-23 and rd-35 drew no dispute. The second annotator also read rd-33 and rd-37 as `keep`
# against the author's `drop`. See `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty/README.md` section 4.
#
# Annotator 1 (author) labelled every case before any decision was run; Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, blind to that label and to the evaluator's answer. Agreement is
# reported in the results directory, and where the paper uses these numbers it states that
# one annotator is a model rather than a second human.
